From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 05:32:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: Allow a fetch to be suspended and resumed

A fetch can only be cancelled. When its player is full, a media element
cancels its fetch and later starts a new Range request. That throws away
the bytes in flight (about 2.5 MB per refill on an OpenHarmony board),
costs a new request and, on HTTP/1.1, a new connection, because a body
that is not read to its end cannot be drained. Gecko suspends the
channel instead, and Chromium defers loading.

Add FetchController::suspend and FetchController::resume. Like a
cancellation, they go through the fetch thread of the content process,
so they stay ordered after the start of the fetch, and reach the
resource thread as CoreResourceMsg::Suspend and CoreResourceMsg::Resume.
There they set the state of the request's CancellationListener, which
now keeps running, suspended or cancelled in a tokio watch channel.

The HTTP network fetch reads the body in a loop that, while the fetch
is suspended, waits for the state to change instead of polling the
body, so that TCP or HTTP/2 flow control holds back the server. Data
received before the suspension is still delivered, and after a resume
the body continues with the next byte. A cancellation wakes the loop at
once, also while it waits for data from the network; before, it was
only noticed when the next chunk arrived. If the connection fails or
closes while the fetch is suspended, the fetch ends after the resume as
an interrupted body does. The loop keeps what it did before: media
bodies are not kept, and a cancellation marks the response aborted
under the body lock.

Net bounds suspension itself, since it learns of no content process
that goes away and an unread body never sees its connection close. A
fetch that stays suspended for longer than the new pref
network_fetch_suspend_timeout_ms (60 s) ends with a network error, as
if its connection was lost. At most two fetches per origin are
suspended at once in each HTTP state, and a further suspension is
ignored: the fetches to an origin share an HTTP/2 connection, and every
suspended stream holds its stream window of the connection window, 2 of
5 MiB with hyper's defaults, so three would stall every other request
to the origin. A suspended fetch that is redirected moves to the new
origin, or is resumed if that has no place left. Suspension is advisory
and only takes effect while a body is read from the network: cache
hits, which are delivered in one chunk, data, file, blob and protocol
handler responses, and fetches that ended ignore it.

A fetch that waits in the HTTP cache for a body that another fetch is
still receiving treats a suspension of that fetch like an abort, and
goes to the network instead of waiting for the resume.

The responses of the redirects, authentication retries and CORS
preflight of a fetch share its listener, so while it is suspended, the
unread bodies of such responses hold their connection until the resume
or the deadline instead of being drained.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: unit tests with servers that stream a 64 MiB body and count
what they hand to the connection: a suspended fetch holds back the
server and receives the whole body intact after the resume, over
HTTP/1.1 and over HTTP/2 (TLS with ALPN, a new test server); a
cancellation ends a suspended fetch, and one that waits for a stalled
server, within a second; a fetch whose server closes the connection
while it is suspended ends after the resume with what was sent; the
deadline ends suspended fetches with a network error and frees their
places; a third suspension per origin is ignored and its server keeps
sending; fetches waiting in the HTTP cache for a suspended response go
to the network; FetchController suspends and resumes a fetch through
the fetch thread and the resource thread, and does nothing after the
end.

Validation: `./mach test-unit -p servo-net`: 412 tests pass (403 before), also with the suspension tests in one process. While a fetch was suspended, the server handed 4.9 MiB of a 64 MiB body to the connection over HTTP/1.1 and 2.1 MiB over HTTP/2. `./mach test-wpt --release --processes 6 --timeout-multiplier 2` fetch/api/basic fetch/api/abort fetch/range fetch/http-cache xhr html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements: 1034 tests, 1027 as expected; the other seven behave the same with the parent commit when run alone three times.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implemented, tested and measured by the agent, reworked after a review by another agent)
